  geopolitics: "The transmission chain from the Strait of Hormuz to this board has not broken, but for the first time it has acquired a timetable, and that is the single most important change in this report. Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi told Al Jazeera that Qatari mediators asked Tehran to shorten its original seven-day proposal and that the two sides settled on two: on day one the United States carries out a package of measures, on day two the Strait reopens and navigation returns to normal. This was the third consecutive day of talks, with Pakistan mediating alongside Qatar, and Iran's security chief Mohsen Rezaei said a letter from Vice President Vance showed a markedly changed tone from Washington, calling it a very positive sign. Crude responded immediately: WTI is 100.473 and minus 3.50%, Brent 109.728 and minus 3.26% and minus 5.25% on the week. Be disciplined about what that price move represents. It is the trimming of a geopolitical premium, not a change in physical supply, which remains constrained while Hormuz flows are disrupted - and that makes it vulnerable to reversal. The conditions Araghchi attached are substantial: lifting recently imposed US sanctions, reinstating the oil waivers granted in June, reducing the US military presence in the region, recognising new shipping routes through the Strait designated by Iran and Oman, ending the conflict on all fronts including Israel's attacks on Lebanon, and releasing frozen funds including those held in Qatar. Several of those, particularly sanctions relief and the recognition of Iranian-designated routes, are difficult to accept quickly. Secretary of State Rubio has confirmed the talks continue but said he is not aware of a timeframe; President Trump said negotiations are going fine but that he is in no great hurry; Washington has issued no public response to the plan itself. Meanwhile the Revolutionary Guards said on Sunday that two more tankers struck mines and exploded while transiting without Iranian authorisation, a claim that has not been independently verified, and other supply risks persist in renewed Ukrainian attacks on Russian refineries and in speculation about US strikes after Tuesday's midterms. The FX read runs in four directions, and the ordering has changed from this file's previous version. First and most urgently, Canada. The Bank of Canada hiked on October 28 explicitly to reduce the risk that persistently high energy prices spread into broader inflation. That is the premise this morning's headline attacks directly, and the December meeting was already a coin flip - National Bank put it near 50% straight after the decision, Macklem said there is no predetermined path, and analysts have been explicit that an energy decline would release the pressure to move again. The Canadian dollar therefore comes down from plus 2 to plus 1. Critically, USD/CAD at 1.39813 and plus 0.06% has not reflected any of this, which means the risk sits ahead of the price rather than behind it. Second, the rate complex more broadly. Four of this board's eight central banks named energy as a reason for tightening - the Bank of England cited prolonged Middle East conflict and higher crude for rising UK CPI, the RBA named higher global energy prices following supply disruptions, the ECB warned that elevated energy prices could fuel inflation while weighing on activity, and the SNB attributed the rise in Swiss inflation mainly to energy. The two near-dated decisions, the RBA on Tuesday and the Bank of England on Thursday, are too close for this week's crude move to change; they will be delivered. What this hits is the guidance behind them, which is why both press conferences matter more than both decisions. Third, the haven and metals side, where today inverted Friday. Gold rose 0.94% to 3912.36 and silver 2.87% to 50.309 while oil fell - that is a disinflation bounce off a one-year low working through the implied terminal rate, not a haven bid, and it is capped by a December Fed hike priced between 71% and 85%. The yen, which was Friday's only functioning haven, gave the move straight back and is climbing toward 158 with the Ministry of Finance now the nearer catalyst than the Bank of Japan. The franc firmed only modestly. Fourth, the correlation warning, which is unchanged and now more acute. USOIL, USD/CAD, CAD/CHF, AUD/CAD and the entire European rate complex all sit on top of one variable: whether Washington answers Iran's conditions. These are not independent positions, and a 4H book should size them as one. This report has moved USOIL from LONG to RANGE and cut Canada by a point rather than flipping either, because the plan requires an American acceptance that has not come, from a President who says he is in no great hurry, while mines are reportedly still detonating in the lanes."
  ,
